[00:08.46]At 7.30 am on 22 January 1943, the people of Spearfish in South Dakota, US woke up to find the temperature outside was a freezing -20°C.
[00:29.98]Two minutes later, thermometers were showing a much warmer 7°C – a rise of 27°C in 120 seconds.
[00:36.80]But it didn’t stop there.
[00:39.06]The temperature continued to go up and by 9 am it was 12°C.
[00:45.70]Just as the people started to think about enjoying a warm winter’s day, the temperature fell 16°C in just under half an hour back down to -4°C and the residents had to put their coats back on!
[01:04.30]It was the most dramatic temperature change in the history of American weather.
[01:11.58]The village of Capracotta is in the mountains near the Adriatic coast of Italy.
[01:17.50]It gets a lot of snow in the winter.
[01:19.48]But on 5 March 2015 its 1,000 inhabitants saw 256 cm of snowfall in 18 hours.
[01:31.14]It was the biggest snowfall ever in a day.
[01:34.71]One photo taken on the day shows a woman in a house shaking hands through the window with her neighbour in the street.
[01:43.66]The neighbour is standing on top of the snow.
[01:46.68]The woman is on the second floor of her home!
[01:51.56]Do you like long sunny days?
[01:54.65]Then the best city in the world to live in is Reykjavik.
[01:59.66]21 June is the longest day of the year in the northern hemisphere and the Icelandic capital gets 21 hours and 45 minutes of daylight.
[02:11.80]The sun hardly ever goes down.
[02:14.93]However, Reykjavik could also be the worst place to be.
[02:20.02]On the shortest day of the year, 21 December, the sun hardly rises there and people get only four hours and seven minutes of daylight.
[02:31.44]But during winter nights, you can often see the Northern Lights – one of the world’s most beautiful natural events.
